pmap_sync_page_data_phys and its twin: pp << PAGE_SHIFT is a 32-bit shift and drops the high bits of a physical address at or above 4 GiB (the S9's DRAM starts at 0x880000000), which phystokv() then rejects; ptoa() widens first.
A fix to Apple's xnu (APSL 2.0 like this series), taken from UniXNU's patches/xnu-12377/zzx-pmap-sync-64bit-pa.patch (github.com/UniXNU/UniXNU, BSD-2-Clause-Patent, pinned at a9ec98c, 8 November 2026).
xnu-8796 already widens (`(vm_offset_t) pp << PAGE_SHIFT`), so this is for the ios-26 base only.
Their licence notice: patches/xnu-12377/LICENSE.UniXNU.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
index 2507221..eac82ee 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
@@ -7244,7 +7244,9 @@ void
 pmap_sync_page_attributes_phys(
 	ppnum_t pp)
 {
-	flush_dcache((vm_offset_t) (pp << PAGE_SHIFT), PAGE_SIZE, TRUE);
+	/* ptoa() widens before the shift: pp << PAGE_SHIFT is a 32-bit shift and drops the high bits of physical
+	 * addresses at or above 4 GiB (all of DRAM on boards whose memory starts there), which phystokv() then rejects. */
+	flush_dcache((vm_offset_t) ptoa(pp), PAGE_SIZE, TRUE);
 }
 
 #if CONFIG_COREDUMP
